--- body ---

## Context

Fourth tranche (tracker 0550): the compute stages. The micro-batch
becomes the schedulable unit everywhere, sized by the ratified quantum.

**The embed worker holds no model** (DECISIONS.md 2026-09-02, entry C of "the
writer leaves the query process…", and the same day's standing correction).
One copy of the model is resident on the machine, per embedder generation,
behind the embedding service ticket 0575 builds; SPEC.md §5.2.5 carries the
shape. This tranche was scoped around a worker that loaded the passage model,
and that premise is gone: embedding is a call over the transport-neutral
interface, the worker's peak is one token-budget batch plus the streamed decode
rather than a model plus a batch, and the worker never spawns the service —
a service spawned by the `nice 19` worker would embed every query at idle
priority. What the tranche keeps is everything that was never about model
residency: the quantum autotune, the claim TTL, the sidecar write ordering and
generation stamping.

The worker's separate process is ruled kept on a new justification (entry B of
the same ruling): the streaming decode of a very large attachment lives in the
fetch path, and fetch failure is what a crash boundary is for. That boundary
isolates memory only if something bounds the process, which is why the heap
limit below is an Action here rather than a remark.

## Relevant files

- SPEC.md §5.2.5 (quantum ~1 s ratified, EWMA autotune with per-step
  clamp, fixed-cost floor, memory ceiling; claim TTL as a multiple of the
  quantum; sidecar ordering: append, fsync, then commit the row; the
  transport-neutral embedding interface and its handshake; the worker at
  `nice 19` under a heap limit at minimum and a cgroup where the platform
  has one), SPEC.md §5.2.9 (the worker's peak by process class after the
  re-cut), DECISIONS.md 2026-09-01 (quantum ruling, slow/stuck split) and
  2026-09-02 (the topology), bench/results/0481-gpu-anomaly (flat-throughput
  finding the autotune wording rests on).
- Ticket 0575 — the embedding service this worker calls. Not a Blocked-by:
  see the log entry of this date. Ticket 0565 — passage geometry, which is
  not this tranche's.

## Actions

1. Dispatch of embed orders, keyed idempotent derivations: a small input
   (record, note, annotation) carries its text outright; a body-text
   order carries an entry-sized RANGE over stored text, which the worker
   reads back read-only and slices. There is no chunk stage here — see
   the 2026-09-01 log entry and 0565.
2. Embed worker, holding no model: each micro-batch is a call to the
   embedding service over the transport-neutral interface (SPEC.md §5.2.5),
   never an in-process load, and the worker has no code path that spawns the
   service. Batch packed to a token budget, length-sorted; size autotuned to
   the quantum (EWMA, step clamp, floor, ceiling); yields between
   micro-batches (activity file, holder check, queue re-read).
3. Worker process bounds, since the process boundary isolates memory only if
   something bounds the process: `nice 19`, a heap limit at minimum, a cgroup
   where the platform has one. The peak this ticket must hold to is one
   token-budget batch plus the streamed decode. The RSS assertion the ruling
   demands is that the worker is KILLED at the bound, not that its peak sat
   under it; the gate that asserts it is ticket 0026's, and this ticket makes
   the bound real for it to assert against.
4. Claim TTL at the ratified multiple of the quantum; expired claims
   reclaimable; duplicate compute bounded to one micro-batch — test it,
   don't assert it.
5. Sidecar writes per SPEC's ordering; generation stamping. Generations are
   the embedder generations SPEC.md §5.2.7 admits two of; the stamp must
   survive the service serving both.

## Test

- **Built first, before the embed worker**: the byte-identical MiniLM check.
  Tracker 0550 states the invariant — the incumbent MiniLM entry reproduces
  its old vectors and keys byte for byte — and ticket 0568 went looking for
  it and found no such check anywhere in the suite. Tranches 1 to 3 never
  needed one; this is the first tranche that touches a vector. Written before
  the code it validates, because a check written after tends to be written
  against what the code does. It runs THROUGH the embedding interface, not
  around it, so it keeps holding when 0491 swaps what sits behind the socket.
- Red first: on the injected clock with a stubbed engine of programmable
  per-item cost — the stub standing in for the service at the transport, since
  the worker has no engine of its own — the autotuned size converges to the
  quantum for two synthetic devices (fast/slow) and oscillation stays inside
  the step clamp when the cost curve is made non-monotonic.

## Verification

- [ ] The byte-identical MiniLM check exists as a test, was demonstrated red
      once, and runs through the embedding interface
- [ ] A worker killed mid-batch: claim expires at the ratified TTL, another
      finishes it, exactly one commit (CAS guard)
- [ ] The worker loads no model on any code path, and has no path that spawns
      the embedding service — asserted, with a positive control that wires an
      in-process load and fails
- [ ] The worker is killed at its heap bound rather than growing past it, and
      its peak under a large streamed decode is a batch plus the decode

## Invariants

- Vector fingerprint handshake per SPEC §5.2.5, which now crosses a process
  boundary; the client rejects a mismatch before reading or writing a vector.
- Upstream tests green.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The fork branch is cut from `conductor-integration`, not from tranche 2
      (ticket 0568; 0550's Context, amended)
- [ ] Both stages merged on a fork branch, convergence + TTL tests green
- [ ] The byte-identical MiniLM check merged with them, and the invariant
      tracker 0550 asserts is no longer untested
